{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786209977,"nonce":"SJL08ePmgO/+8zW26AYdEUXC8rU=","title":"**What is wrong.** `internal/enrichment/` has no test file of any kind. That","message":"covers the enricher registry, the five shipped enrichers (`alerthistory`,\n`promrule`, `relatedalerts`, `runbook`, `silencematch`), the enrichment cache and\nits expiry sweep (`internal/enrichment/repository/cache.go:109`), and\n`Enrichment.Validate()` — which `review-conformance.md` §5 flags as an optional\nvalidator on a type with no constructor, \"exported fields, freely constructible\ninvalid\".\n\n**Why it matters.** Enrichment is what makes an oto alert more useful than the\nAlertmanager message it came from — the rule expression, the recent history, the\nmatching silence. It is rendered into the Slack card and the alert detail view, so\na wrong or stale enrichment is a wrong statement shown to a human deciding whether\nto act. A failing enricher must degrade to \"not available\" rather than to a\nmissing or misleading field, and nothing checks that today.\n\n**Where.** `internal/enrichment/{domain,service,repository,enrichers}/`.\n\n**Done when.** Each enricher has tests for the found, not-found, upstream-error\nand timeout cases; the registry is tested for an unknown enricher id and for one\nenricher failing without taking the others down; the cache is tested for hit, miss\nand expiry; and a service-level test asserts an enrichment failure never blocks\nthe notification.","files":null}]}